{"name":"Pomegranate Seeds","hex":"#95313a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pomegranate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fruit of the Punica granatum, about the size of an orange with a thick, hard, reddish skin enclosing many seeds, each with an edible pink or red pulp tasting both sweet and tart.","The shrub or small tree that bears the fruit."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a colour like that of the pulp or skin of a pomegranate fruit; dark red or orange-red."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pomegranate"},{"term":"seeds","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of seed"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of seed"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seeds"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pomegranate","blocks":[{"text":"The pomegranate is a fruit-bearing, deciduous shrub in the family Lythraceae, subfamily Punicoideae, that grows to between 1.5–5 metres (5–16 ft) tall. Rich in symbolic and mythological associations in many cultures, it originated in the region spanning the Caucasus and the Iranian plateau — including modern-day Iran, Turkmenistan, Afghanistan and Pakistan. Pomegranate was first domesticated by ancient Iranians in the Iranian plateau and nearby regions about 5,000 years ago. It is extensively cultivated for its fruit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Pomegranate_Juice_%282019%29.jpg/330px-Pomegranate_Juice_%282019%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomegranate","wikidata":"Q13188"},{"title":"Seed","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a seed is a plant structure containing an embryo and stored nutrients in a protective coat called a testa. More generally, the term seed means anything that can be sown, which may include seed and husk or tuber. Seeds are the product of the ripened ovule, after the embryo sac is fertilized by sperm from pollen, forming a zygote. The embryo within a seed develops from the zygote and grows within the mother plant to a certain size before growth is halted."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BE%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F%D0%BD.jpg/330px-%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BE%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F%D0%BD.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seed","wikidata":"Q40763"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Pomegranate_fruit_-_whole_and_piece_with_arils.jpg/250px-Pomegranate_fruit_-_whole_and_piece_with_arils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Pomegranate_fruit_-_whole_and_piece_with_arils.jpg/500px-Pomegranate_fruit_-_whole_and_piece_with_arils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Pomegranate_fruit_-_whole_and_piece_with_arils.jpg/250px-Pomegranate_fruit_-_whole_and_piece_with_arils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APomegranate_fruit_-_whole_and_piece_with_arils.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Flower_of_Pomegranate.jpg/250px-Flower_of_Pomegranate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Flower_of_Pomegranate.jpg/500px-Flower_of_Pomegranate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Flower_of_Pomegranate.jpg/250px-Flower_of_Pomegranate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=